3.2 Transcript preprocessing
The ASR output arrives flat: no speaker labels, poor or absent punctuation, speaker_labels_present: false in the schema. Three recurring defects were characterised from real transcripts:
Bank name garbling — Kotak transcribed as "Medra", "Ventra", "Maintha", "Mainina", "paandr", "mahendr", "kodak".
Hallucination runs — nannannannan sequences emitted over silence.
camelCase glue points — segment joins where both the space and the sentence-ending period are lost, e.g. chaahoongiNahin.
Deliverables built and tested:
preprocess_transcript.py — four ordered operations:
Replace nan hallucination runs with [PAUSE] markers
Split at camelCase glue points and sentence punctuation
Break over-long lines at Hinglish discourse markers (Haan, Ji, Nahin, Ok, Achchha, Thik hai, …)
Number the lines
pl_transcript_preprocessing.ipynb — the fuller pipeline: S3 integration placeholders, validation, call profile building, chunking with overlap, diagnostics, and an offset integrity check.
3.3 Speaker attribution
Audio-based diarization (pyannote and similar) was ruled out — recordings are mono-channel and the time cost was not justified. Attribution is therefore text-only, inferred from linguistic signals.
speaker-attribution-prompt.md — finalised and confirmed production-ready. Signals used, in rough order of strength:
Possessive direction — aapka vs mera. The strongest generic signal.
Right-party confirmation digit strings — agent-side.
Competitor bank mentions — customer-side.
Polite imperative endings — -kijie / -dijie, agent-side.
A worked example is included in the prompt.
Gender inference was explicitly removed from the prompt.
3.4 System architecture
A full technical reference was produced: pl-call-analytics-technical-doc-aws.md, revised twice as platform facts were confirmed.
Stage design agreed:
Ingest (no LLM) — PII redaction with a reversible mapping stored separately, turn numbering, speaker role assignment, trimming pre-connect dialer audio via recording offset.
Triage router (rules only) — turn count, engagement patterns, hangup signatures; sorts into no-contact / short-refusal / substantive. Targets removal of ~60% of calls before any model call. This is a cost lever, not a quality lever, and sits upstream of all inference.
Deterministic metrics (no LLM) — talk-time ratio, longest monologue, interruption count, dead air, speech rate, question count. Shippable to ops on its own.
LLM stages, ordered by value: outcome → compliance → objections and intent → pitch dimensions → fact extraction. Strict output schema per stage, required evidence turns, validation with one retry then dead-letter, output stamped with model / prompt version / knowledge version.
Verifier — independent of the labeller. Takes a claim plus its cited turns and judges whether those turns actually support the claim. Run on compliance flags first, where false positives are most expensive.
Merge and contradiction rules — combine stage outputs into one call record, catch contradictions (e.g. outcome says converted while objection says hard refusal).
Review screen — flagged claim, cited turns, audio, confirm/reject with reason.
Storage model: a calls table, an insights table with typed JSON per stage, an evidence table linking insights to turn numbers. Writes idempotent on (call_id, stage, version) so re-runs replace rather than duplicate.
3.5 Labelling governance
Codebook — a versioned rulebook with anchored definitions, git-tracked, with a standing adjudication log. Shared source of truth for both human annotators and prompts.
OKF — a markdown file-and-folder convention keeping product facts and rules in one place, readable by prompts and humans alike.
Gold set — pilot first, compute per-label agreement; anything below 0.6 means the definition is broken and the codebook gets fixed before re-piloting. Main run: one annotator per call with 20–25% double-labelled throughout to watch drift, third-person adjudication, every adjudication written back as a codebook edge case. Then freeze, version, split dev/test, lock the test half away.
